1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,461
Open your own capstone package next to this lecture, not after it.

2
00:00:04,461 --> 00:00:09,444
The seven rows on the next slide are specific enough to check in the next twenty

3
00:00:09,444 --> 00:00:14,613
minutes, and honest enough that a few of them will probably fail on the first pass.

4
00:00:14,613 --> 00:00:17,354
That's the exercise working, not a bad sign.

5
00:00:17,354 --> 00:00:22,150
Notice this rubric doesn't ask "is it good," the way most self-review ends up

6
00:00:22,150 --> 00:00:25,140
working when nobody hands you specific criteria.

7
00:00:25,140 --> 00:00:30,309
Every row asks something you can answer yes or no to, the same discipline 3.7 asked

8
00:00:30,309 --> 00:00:31,867
of a PRD's own threshold.

9
00:00:31,867 --> 00:00:36,227
A checklist you can argue with yourself about isn't doing its job yet.

10
00:00:36,227 --> 00:00:41,334
This is also the last hands-on exercise in the entire course, which makes it worth

11
00:00:41,334 --> 00:00:44,386
doing properly rather than rushing to the finish.

12
00:00:44,386 --> 00:00:49,431
Six sections of workshops have been building toward the moment you check your own

13
00:00:49,431 --> 00:00:50,552
work this closely.

14
00:00:50,552 --> 00:00:55,473
Treat it that way, not as a formality between the capstone and the lecture that

15
00:00:55,473 --> 00:00:56,594
closes the course.

16
00:00:57,144 --> 00:01:02,202
Read the golden set row against 11.2's worked example specifically: the exact

17
00:01:02,202 --> 00:01:06,997
fifty-dollar boundary, and the exact injection sentence from 8.2, weren't

18
00:01:06,997 --> 00:01:07,719
decoration.

19
00:01:07,719 --> 00:01:11,398
They were what made that golden set pass this exact row.

20
00:01:11,398 --> 00:01:16,850
If your own set only has clean, obvious cases, this is the row that fails, and it's

21
00:01:16,850 --> 00:01:20,856
the cheapest one to fix before you call the package finished.

22
00:01:20,856 --> 00:01:23,484
Read the risk register row the same way.

23
00:01:23,484 --> 00:01:28,739
A placeholder like "no known issues" in any of the five categories isn't a pass,

24
00:01:28,739 --> 00:01:31,891
it's an unexamined category wearing a pass mark.

25
00:01:31,891 --> 00:01:37,015
11.3's worked example gave even prompt injection, the row already mitigated by

26
00:01:37,015 --> 00:01:39,577
design, a real sentence explaining why.

27
00:01:39,577 --> 00:01:44,306
Every row deserves that same sentence, even the ones that turn out fine.

28
00:01:44,856 --> 00:01:49,354
Which of your own seven rows would you actually bet money still fails on a second

29
00:01:49,354 --> 00:01:49,632
look?

30
00:01:49,632 --> 00:01:52,353
For most first-pass capstones, it's the last one.

31
00:01:52,353 --> 00:01:57,017
A metric tree can look complete with a North Star and three branches while having no

32
00:01:57,017 --> 00:01:59,960
real connection to the cost model two artifacts back.

33
00:01:59,960 --> 00:02:04,403
If your North Star doesn't answer the specific question your cost model's payoff

34
00:02:04,403 --> 00:02:08,901
claim depends on, the tree is decorative, the same failure 11.1's coherence check

35
00:02:08,901 --> 00:02:10,733
warned the whole package against.

36
00:02:11,283 --> 00:02:16,195
Notice both columns can produce a package that looks identical from across the room,

37
00:02:16,195 --> 00:02:18,593
every field filled in, every row present.

38
00:02:18,593 --> 00:02:23,095
The difference only shows up when someone reads closely, which is exactly why

39
00:02:23,095 --> 00:02:26,370
self-assessment is so easy to do badly without noticing.

40
00:02:26,370 --> 00:02:31,224
Grade yourself the way 4.9 asked you to read an eval result: looking for the reason

41
00:02:31,224 --> 00:02:35,375
you'd fail your own package, not confirming the reasons it should pass.

42
00:02:35,375 --> 00:02:40,229
That's an uncomfortable habit to build against your own work specifically, and it's

43
00:02:40,229 --> 00:02:44,498
the entire value of doing this exercise seriously instead of skimming it.

44
00:02:44,498 --> 00:02:48,532
Notice, too, that the box-checking column isn't dishonest on purpose.

45
00:02:48,532 --> 00:02:49,936
It's usually just tired.

46
00:02:49,936 --> 00:02:54,555
By the time a capstone's sixth artifact is finished, skimming your own work and

47
00:02:54,555 --> 00:02:58,181
calling it done is the natural instinct, not a character flaw.

48
00:02:58,181 --> 00:03:02,917
Naming that instinct here is what gives you a real chance of resisting it on this

49
00:03:02,917 --> 00:03:03,736
specific pass.

50
00:03:04,286 --> 00:03:09,348
Say the honest caveat, because a checklist this specific can start to feel like more

51
00:03:09,348 --> 00:03:11,518
certainty than it actually provides.

52
00:03:11,518 --> 00:03:16,460
Every row here checks that something exists and is labelled honestly, not that the

53
00:03:16,460 --> 00:03:19,051
underlying judgement behind it was correct.

54
00:03:19,051 --> 00:03:23,632
Your cost model can pass this rubric's row completely, every number labelled

55
00:03:23,632 --> 00:03:28,272
measured or projected, while still resting on a volume estimate that's wrong.

56
00:03:28,272 --> 00:03:33,334
That gap is exactly what 10.6's follow-up-question test and a second reader are for,

57
00:03:33,334 --> 00:03:36,227
neither of which this rubric can substitute for.

58
00:03:36,227 --> 00:03:41,169
Use this checklist for what it's actually good at: catching the missing threshold,

59
00:03:41,169 --> 00:03:45,810
the placeholder risk row, the North Star with no real connection to anything.

60
00:03:45,810 --> 00:03:50,872
Then find one other person and run 10.6's test on the parts this rubric can't check.

61
00:03:51,422 --> 00:03:56,240
Do this now, while your own package is still open, not as something to come back to

62
00:03:56,240 --> 00:03:59,723
later once the feeling of being finished has already set in.

63
00:03:59,723 --> 00:04:04,367
Most people find one or two genuine fails on a first honest pass, most often the

64
00:04:04,367 --> 00:04:09,185
golden set's boundary cases or the metric tree's connection back to the cost model.

65
00:04:09,185 --> 00:04:14,003
Fixing either one usually takes under an hour, which is a small price for a package

66
00:04:14,003 --> 00:04:15,977
that can survive more than a skim.

67
00:04:16,527 --> 00:04:18,268
One thing before you move on.

68
00:04:18,268 --> 00:04:22,892
Run the checklist honestly, today, while the package is fresh enough that you

69
00:04:22,892 --> 00:04:24,934
remember why you made each choice.

70
00:04:24,934 --> 00:04:29,318
Then fix what failed before you're tempted to add a new artifact instead.

71
00:04:29,318 --> 00:04:34,302
A seventh artifact doesn't rescue a package with one real gap in the six it already

72
00:04:34,302 --> 00:04:34,542
has.

73
00:04:34,542 --> 00:04:38,265
Next lecture is the last one: where to go next, and thank you.
